# Every drawer is append-only: two sessions independently add lines at
# the end of a file. A normal merge sees a conflict ("both changed the
# last line") and stops — while the right answer is always the same:
# keep both.
#
# `merge=union` does exactly that: git concatenates both sides with no
# conflict markers. For JSONL this is correct, because every line
# stands on its own and the order does not matter — each entry carries
# its own `ts`.
#
# Do NOT use this for structured files (JSON, YAML): union would glue
# two halves of an object together and destroy the file.
*.jsonl merge=union

# LF in the working tree on every platform.
#
# Several tests parse this repository's own text: the README's command
# block, the capability tables, the count claims. Their patterns are
# written with \n, because that is what the files contain. On a Windows
# checkout git used to convert them to CRLF, the patterns stopped
# matching, and the tests reported "the README has no command block at
# all" — about a file that was perfectly fine.
#
# The shell hooks need LF regardless; PowerShell and Node read LF
# without complaint. So there is no platform that wants CRLF here.
* text=auto eol=lf
